    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the breakdown. i'm in portland so it's been pretty rainy lately. i was looking at some granular stuff at the garden center but i'm confused about the application. how do i know if i should be using a high-nitrogen one versus something else, especially if the soil is already pretty wet? also, is there a certain time of year i should be applying these to avoid just washing them away?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Indeed, Portland's rainfall could make your grass grow denser due to its consistent watering with rain, so it's essential to choose the right nutrients accordingly. As your grass competes for resources with weeds, apply a balanced fertilizer early in the spring. This promotes robust growth ahead of weed competition, especially since a small-scale weed invasion early spring won’t bog down a new grass plant. Standard lawn fertilizer provides an optimal 30-30-30 ratio, helping with growth, feeding roots, and improving drought resistance.\n\nThe timing of your application is also crucial. Apply your fertilizer once a month throughout the growing season, which lasts from early spring until fall. Delayed first application, particularly in harsh weather with rain, can affect root development. Volunteer leaf lawnmowers (overgrown clumps of grass left behind by your mower) may distract your lawn plants from absorbing nutrients. For Florida lawns, here's a straightforward schedule:\n\nThe Lawn Maintainer - April 1: Apply the high-nitrogen fertilizer. Wet-dry cycles prompt an early first application. Its effects last through July, when cool temperatures halt growth.\nMid-June 15th: Apply again for 1/2 lb. per application, giving early spring and monthly applications.\nThe Lawn",
      "source": "target",
